{
  "case_id": "2019-18-217",
  "document_hash": "71b68fec51fe737c782f16ec87c00d83633dae3f433699380ac80f5028843b98",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2019-18-217",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "71b68fec51fe737c782f16ec87c00d83633dae3f433699380ac80f5028843b98",
      "evidence_id": "ev-71b68fec51fe-0000-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Fifteen years ago, Lee Malvo was tried, convicted, and sentenced for his role in the D.C. sniper attacks. Almost a decade later, Malvo sought federal habeas relief, relying exclusively on the new rule announced by this Court in Miller versus Alabama."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2019-18-217",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "71b68fec51fe737c782f16ec87c00d83633dae3f433699380ac80f5028843b98",
      "evidence_id": "ev-71b68fec51fe-0000-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 6,
        "start": 6
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: Mr. Heytens, could we back up a little and explain to me why these sentences are not mandatory? I mean, the jury had only two choices, death or life without parole. And nobody seemed to have appreciated at the time of Malvo's convictions that there was any discretion."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2019-18-217",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "71b68fec51fe737c782f16ec87c00d83633dae3f433699380ac80f5028843b98",
      "evidence_id": "ev-71b68fec51fe-0000-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 8,
        "start": 8
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOTOMAYOR: But did the judge know he could, given that there was no history of doing it? I think that's -- that's the position of the SG in this case."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "United States",
      "case_id": "2019-18-217",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "71b68fec51fe737c782f16ec87c00d83633dae3f433699380ac80f5028843b98",
      "evidence_id": "ev-71b68fec51fe-0000-0001",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 22,
        "start": 22
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. FEIGIN: Thank you, Mr. Chief Justice, and may it please the Court: Malvo is arguing that his life without parole sentences for his murders are"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kavanaugh",
      "case_id": "2019-18-217",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "71b68fec51fe737c782f16ec87c00d83633dae3f433699380ac80f5028843b98",
      "evidence_id": "ev-71b68fec51fe-0001-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 23,
        "start": 23
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Now, Justice Kavanaugh, you asked how we know that a discretionary scheme -- the existence of a discretionary scheme is sufficient to protect against the substantive right that Montgomery finds that Miller recognizes."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2019-18-217",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "71b68fec51fe737c782f16ec87c00d83633dae3f433699380ac80f5028843b98",
      "evidence_id": "ev-71b68fec51fe-0001-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 33,
        "start": 33
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: Mr. Feigin, I would like to ask you about the government's change in position because, as I understood it, the government originally argued that juveniles -- juveniles sentenced to life without parole must be resentenced after Miller and Montgomery, whether life without parole is mandatory or imposed as a matter of discretion."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2019-18-217",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "71b68fec51fe737c782f16ec87c00d83633dae3f433699380ac80f5028843b98",
      "evidence_id": "ev-71b68fec51fe-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 44,
        "start": 44
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "What are you -- what are you asking for, all of those other systems, post-Jones, that let or tell judges to consider age but don't say in accordance with Miller and Montgomery? Don't we presume that they know the law and follow it? That those judges --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "MS. Spinelli",
      "case_id": "2019-18-217",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "71b68fec51fe737c782f16ec87c00d83633dae3f433699380ac80f5028843b98",
      "evidence_id": "ev-71b68fec51fe-0002-0007",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 44,
        "start": 44
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "If a judge sentences a juvenile under one of the post-Montgomery statutes that sets out the factors that are articulated in Miller and Montgomery, then, yes, I think it might be"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kagan",
      "case_id": "2019-18-217",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "71b68fec51fe737c782f16ec87c00d83633dae3f433699380ac80f5028843b98",
      "evidence_id": "ev-71b68fec51fe-0002-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 45,
        "start": 45
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "It sets those out and -- and requires courts to evaluate them?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kavanaugh",
      "case_id": "2019-18-217",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "71b68fec51fe737c782f16ec87c00d83633dae3f433699380ac80f5028843b98",
      "evidence_id": "ev-71b68fec51fe-0002-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 47,
        "start": 47
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Kagan's question for a second. In a discretionary regime where the sentencer is required to consider certain factors or even if not, it's just a discretionary regime, the defense counsel in any case where a juvenile's facing life without parole as a possibility is, of course, I would think, you would agree, any competent defense counsel is going to argue the youth to the sentencing judge. Do you agree with that?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Gorsuch",
      "case_id": "2019-18-217",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "71b68fec51fe737c782f16ec87c00d83633dae3f433699380ac80f5028843b98",
      "evidence_id": "ev-71b68fec51fe-0002-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 50,
        "start": 50
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "if there were a requirement of a finding --a substantive right to a finding of incorrigibility before the -- the sentence of life without parole were permissible under the Eighth Amendment, wouldn't it follow also that there's a Sixth Amendment right under Apprendi to have a jury decide that rather than a judge?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2019-18-217",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "71b68fec51fe737c782f16ec87c00d83633dae3f433699380ac80f5028843b98",
      "evidence_id": "ev-71b68fec51fe-0003-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 66,
        "start": 66
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE ALITO: Is not now or was not at the time?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. Spinelli",
      "case_id": "2019-18-217",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "71b68fec51fe737c782f16ec87c00d83633dae3f433699380ac80f5028843b98",
      "evidence_id": "ev-71b68fec51fe-0003-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 66,
        "start": 66
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MS. SPINELLI: -- this is -- you know, if one is permanently incorrigible, that's a permanent quality. So it certainly is relevant on resentencing what someone has done since they committed the crime. They may well have, you know, been able to provide evidence based on what they did after the crime, that they are not, in fact, permanently incorrigible."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2019-18-217",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "71b68fec51fe737c782f16ec87c00d83633dae3f433699380ac80f5028843b98",
      "evidence_id": "ev-71b68fec51fe-0003-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 66,
        "start": 66
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE ALITO: So, if he can demonstrate, as a result of good behavior in prison, for example, that he has been rehabilitated, then he must be released?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. Spinelli",
      "case_id": "2019-18-217",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "71b68fec51fe737c782f16ec87c00d83633dae3f433699380ac80f5028843b98",
      "evidence_id": "ev-71b68fec51fe-0003-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 66,
        "start": 66
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MS. SPINELLI: No. No, absolutely not. That's one piece of evidence that the sentencer can consider. The sentencer then can decide what is the sentence going to be."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. Spinelli",
      "case_id": "2019-18-217",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "71b68fec51fe737c782f16ec87c00d83633dae3f433699380ac80f5028843b98",
      "evidence_id": "ev-71b68fec51fe-0003-0004",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 67,
        "start": 67
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "All of the arguments that they've raised were also raised in Montgomery, and the Court declined to adopt them, and it shouldn't change here."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Toby J. Heytens",
      "case_id": "2019-18-217",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "71b68fec51fe737c782f16ec87c00d83633dae3f433699380ac80f5028843b98",
      "evidence_id": "ev-71b68fec51fe-0003-0005",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 68,
        "start": 68
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. HEYTENS: So I'd just like to address three points: what Miller requires, the shifting nature of Malvo's arguments, and why this matters."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2019-18-217",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "71b68fec51fe737c782f16ec87c00d83633dae3f433699380ac80f5028843b98",
      "evidence_id": "ev-71b68fec51fe-0003-0006",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 69,
        "start": 69
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOTOMAYOR: And how about the case they cited where counsel did raise this argument about the youth and the judge said, I have no power?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Toby J. Heytens",
      "case_id": "2019-18-217",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "71b68fec51fe737c782f16ec87c00d83633dae3f433699380ac80f5028843b98",
      "evidence_id": "ev-71b68fec51fe-0003-0007",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 71,
        "start": 71
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. HEYTENS: Thank you. So I think under Teague, it's clear as day that for Mr. Malvo to get retroactive relief he needs a new rule."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Source extraction",
      "case_id": "2019-18-217",
